WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.110
When Star Wars: The Last Jedi hits theaters
on December 15, it's guaranteed to be one

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:08.340
of the biggest box office hits of the year,
if not all time.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.430
So why are some theaters flat-out refusing
to screen the movie at all?

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:16.430
According to a report by The Wall Street Journal,
Disney is imposing harsh financial requirements

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:20.230
on the theaters they'll partner with to screen
The Last Jedi — terms that many theater

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:22.730
owners are calling the "most onerous they
have ever seen."

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.780
Typically, movie studios take 55 to 60 percent
of ticket sales for a first run film, with

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.160
that percentage dropping the longer the movie
is out.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.140
However, as one film insider told The Wall
Street Journal, Disney is currently "in the

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.111
most powerful position any studio has ever
been in" thanks to the fact that the last

00:00:38.111 --> 00:00:42.979
film in the series, 2015's Star Wars: The
Force Awakens, is by far the biggest domestic

00:00:42.979 --> 00:00:44.530
box office hit in history.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:50.129
As a result, Disney is asking for an unprecedented
65 percent of the box office take from all

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:51.570
participating theaters.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.260
Needless to say, the studio has significantly
altered the usual deal.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.520
"I have altered the deal! Pray I don't alter it any further."

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.170
"This deal is getting worse all the time."

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:05.070
But wait, the deal does get worse!

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:08.960
Any theater that wants to show The Last Jedi
also has to agree to screen it for a full

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.610
four weeks at a minimum in their biggest auditorium.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:15.890
Plus, Disney is also imposing an uncommon
penalty for violating these terms.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.820
Should Disney deem any theater to be in violation
of the agreement, the agreement says that

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:23.460
Disney is then allowed to take an additional
5 percent of ticket sales from the theater

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:28.210
— meaning that for four weeks, 70 percent
of all money earned from The Last Jedi in

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.300
a theater would go straight to Disney.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.410
Violations, according to Disney, would include
pulling even one screening from a theater's

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.930
schedule, or using marketing materials for
the movie before Disney gives the go-ahead.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:42.850
While these terms still are harsh for massive
chains like AMC or Cinemark, the conditions

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.560
really hurt smaller theaters, serving small
populations.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:49.940
And some independent theater operators are
considering fighting back by boycotting The

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.620
Last Jedi entirely.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:56.120
One cinema owner who operates a single-screen
theater noted that in his small town, everyone

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.870
who wanted to see The Last Jedi would see
it in the first two weeks, leaving the owner

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:03.870
stuck playing the movie for the last two weeks
to what would eventually be an empty room.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:08.200
For this trouble, the theater would keep only
35 percent of the increasingly meager profits

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.120
from it all — or 30 percent, if Disney decides
they broke a rule.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.760
Considering the record-breaking success of
The Force Awakens, it's easy to see why Disney

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:16.909
would want more money.

00:02:16.909 --> 00:02:20.950
But as crazy as it sounds, it might actually
make more financial sense for some theaters

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:25.730
not to screen The Last Jedi at all rather
than give in to Disney's demands.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:26.730
Thanks for watching!

